---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifeSizeConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifeSizeConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="ring-size-converter">
  <UnitsLifeSizeConverter client:load mode="ring" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Each ring size is one measurement</h2>
    <p>
      Ring sizing, unlike shoe and clothing sizing, has a physical basis that all systems share: the inside
      diameter of the band. The national size systems are labels for that diameter.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>US and Canada: a numeric scale where each full size adds 0.8128 mm of diameter (about 2.55 mm of circumference).</li>
      <li>UK, Ireland, Australia: letters, about two per US size, starting at A.</li>
      <li>EU / ISO 8653: the inside circumference in millimetres. A US 7 is 54.4 mm, so it is a European 54.</li>
      <li>Japan and China: their own numeric scale, from about 1 to 27.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Circumference is diameter times &pi;, so conversion between the diameter and circumference columns is
      exact. Conversion between the national labels is a lookup, which the ring size converter chart on this
      page gives.
    </p>

    <h2>Two reliable ways to measure ring size at home</h2>
    <h3>Measure a ring you already own</h3>
    <p>
      This is the most accurate home method, because a finished ring is easier to measure than a soft,
      changing finger. Calibrate the on-screen sizer with a bank card: every ID-1 payment card is exactly
      85.60 mm wide, which lets the page calculate how many pixels your display uses per millimetre. Then put
      the ring over the circle. The inside edge of the band must just touch the line.
    </p>
    <h3>Print the strip</h3>
    <p>
      The printable sizer below the tool draws its circles in real millimetres. Print at exactly 100% scale,
      with "fit to page" and "shrink oversized pages" turned off. Then check the print with a bank card. If
      the card outline is not 85.6 mm wide on the paper, the scale is wrong and so are the circles.
    </p>

    <h2>Why string does not work</h2>
    <p>
      Many people wrap string or paper around a finger and measure it, and the result is usually a half to a
      full size wrong. String stretches, paper compresses, and neither has to pass over the knuckle. A ring
      must clear the knuckle and then sit comfortably on a narrower part of the finger. A circumference
      measurement at the base of the finger cannot capture both limits.
    </p>

    <h2>Fingers change size</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Factor</th><th scope="col">Effect</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Temperature</td><td>Up to half a size smaller when cold, larger when hot</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Time of day</td><td>Largest in the late afternoon and evening</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Salt, alcohol, heat</td><td>Temporary swelling, often a quarter to half a size</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Exercise</td><td>Hands swell during and after; measure at rest</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pregnancy</td><td>Often a full size or more, usually reversing afterwards</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Band width</td><td>A band over 6 mm wide fits tighter; size up a quarter to a half</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Measure two or three times on different days at a normal temperature and use the middle reading. If you
      are between sizes, size up. A ring that will not come off is a worse problem than one that is slightly
      loose, and a jeweller can size a loose ring down or fit it with beads.
    </p>

    <h2>Buying a ring as a surprise</h2>
    <p>
      Borrow a ring the person already wears on the same finger and measure it. Note the finger: the ring
      finger and the middle finger are usually a size apart. If you cannot borrow one, US 6&ndash;6.5 and US
      9&ndash;10 are the most common women&apos;s and men&apos;s sizes. Most jewellers resize once for free
      within a set period; ask before you buy.
    </p>

    <h2>Runs in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      The page computes the chart, the calibration and the printable strip. Your measurements stay on your
      device.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
